PROOF THAT ONLY COMMENT LINES CHANGED.
1. diff -r reviewed copy vs live tools/src lists exactly one file, util.js, with three hunks: 3 comment lines replace 2 (lines 104-105), +2 comment lines (after line 297), +2 comment lines (after line 513). 7 lines added, 2 removed, 542 -> 547 lines, every one of them a `//` line.
2. Every changed line (difflib over both files) matches ^\s*// : none that is not a full-line comment.
3. With all full-line comments stripped from both files the rest is identical, 482 lines each, blank lines included, so no whitespace outside comment lines changed. No trailing whitespace in the new lines; the longest new line is 87 characters (the longest comment line already in the file is 91).
4. I parsed both files with acorn (ecmaVersion latest, sourceType module, the copy in tools/node_modules) and compared the ASTs with positions removed: identical, 39 top-level nodes. So no statement, expression, import or declaration changed.
apply.js (sha256 fdd0e0b6...) and prepare.js (66551a90...) are byte-identical to the reviewed copy. util.js sha256: 8bed1968... -> 463d9b4f4ed891c7f5724d72e13e7ac3f97697b5e45a37cb72773f0484ab451c.

WHAT THE COMMENTS SAY, AND HOW I CHECKED IT (Q3: I did not just repeat the reviewer's probe).
F1, util.js lines 299-300 (stage) and 517-518 (createFolderAllOrNothing): the options of the two fs.writeFileSync(..., { flag: "wx", flush: true }) calls must stay free of `encoding`. Checked three ways on Node v22.21.1. (a) The installed Node's own source, printed with fs.writeFileSync.toString(): the C++ fast path is taken when typeof data === 'string' and options.encoding is 'utf8' or 'utf-8', and it calls binding.writeFileUtf8(path, data, flags, mode) without any flush; only the JS path calls fs.fsyncSync(fd) when flush is set. (b) A probe that counts fs.fsyncSync calls of one writeFileSync of a string: {flag:"wx",flush:true} -> 1 call; the same plus encoding "utf8" or "utf-8" -> 0 calls, file written fine; encoding "latin1" -> 1 call; a Buffer -> 1 call even with encoding "utf8". So the comment names `encoding: "utf8"`, which is what I confirmed. (c) On the real code paths through the real CLI, with a preload that patches fs.fsyncSync (scratch projects only): apply-verdict on first apply 3 calls, on re-apply of all three targets 3, in escalate mode 2, when nothing must change 0; prepare-run 4 (3 source copies + run.json). On a scratch copy of tools/ where encoding: "utf8" was added to both calls, every one of those counts drops to 0 while all commands still exit 0 with the same stdout: exactly the silent regression the comments warn about. For TEST-DESIGNER's row: one fs.fsyncSync per temp file (per changed target; per file in the new run folder), measured by a preload that wraps fs.fsyncSync like the fault-inject module wraps the other methods. I did not mention that row in the code comments, because it does not exist yet.
F6, util.js lines 103-106 (above isOsError): the old closing sentence 'Nothing in this section catches "everything"' is replaced by what the code does: only OS errors (string code and syscall) become E_WRITE lines; any other exception is a program error, rollback and cleanup run, then it is rethrown unchanged with its stack trace (design 5.3, Q6); no catch in this section turns it into E_WRITE or drops it. I re-read every catch block of the section for that last clause: osCall, osError, entryAt, exists, stage, both catches in writeAllOrNothing and all three in createFolderAllOrNothing either handle an OS error or rethrow the exception unchanged, after undo() or the temp-folder removal where something was created. (As the REVIEWER noted in F5, a program error thrown inside the post-commit cleanup loop leaves the remaining .sai-old backups; the comment does not claim otherwise, it follows the wording of design 5.3.)

CHECKS (cd tools, after the edit): npm run format:check exit 0, 'All matched files use Prettier code style!'. npm run lint exit 0 (eslint . --max-warnings 0, no output). npm run test exit 0: tests 115, suites 0, pass 115, fail 0, cancelled 0, skipped 0, todo 0 (the same counts as before the edit). As extra evidence for the "no behaviour change" claim my scratch matrix (the 67 rows W01-W52, W13b, P01-P14 through the real CLI, see the first handoff) was re-run on the edited file: 67 of 67 pass.

